[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is: No, in general Gromov-Witten invariants do NOT simply count [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmorphisms from a curve to a variety. They count stable maps modulo [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mautomorphisms, with virtual fundamental class corrections, and they include [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontributions from nodal domains, multiple covers, etc. They are virtual counts,[0m[38;2;124;124;124mnot naive counts of morphisms.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this more carefully to give a proper proof/explanation.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mGromov-Witten invariants are defined via the moduli space of stable maps $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\overline{M}_{g,n}(X, \beta)$, where $\beta \in H_2(X, \mathbb{Z})$. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minvariant is obtained by integrating against the virtual fundamental class [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$[\overline{M}_{g,n}(X, \beta)]^{vir}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey points why they don't simply count morphisms:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m1. **Virtual fundamental class**: The moduli space is generally not of expected [0m[38;2;124;124;124mdimension; it has a virtual fundamental class of dimension $(1-g)(\dim X - 3) + [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\int_\beta c_1(TX) + n$. The invariant is a virtual count, incorporating [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mobstruction theory.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m2. **Stable maps, not just morphisms**: The objects are stable maps $f: C \to X$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhere $C$ is a nodal curve (prestable curve), not necessarily smooth. So the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdomain is allowed to be nodal, and the map must be stable (finite automorphism [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgroup). This is broader than "morphisms from a curve" if one means a smooth [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcurve.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m3. **Multiple covers and contributions**: For genus 0, when counting rational [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcurves, multiple covers of a single curve contribute, and these contributions [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mare rational numbers (after dividing by automorphisms), not integers counting [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdistinct morphisms.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m4. **Automorphisms**: The count is weighted by $1/|\text{Aut}|$, so it's a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mrational number, not an integer count of morphisms.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m5. **Even when the moduli space is smooth of expected dimension and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124munobstructed**, the invariant counts stable maps (which include maps from nodal [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcurves) weighted by automorphisms, not bare morphisms.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo the answer is: No, Gromov-Witten invariants do not simply count morphisms [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfrom a curve to a variety. They are virtual counts of stable maps from prestable[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(nodal) curves, weighted by automorphisms, defined via the virtual fundamental [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mclass.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHowever, there are special cases where they do coincide with naive counts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- When $X$ is convex (e.g., homogeneous spaces $G/P$) and genus 0, the moduli [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mspace is smooth of expected dimension, and the invariant counts stable maps [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(still weighted by automorphisms, still allowing nodal domains).[0m
